# The ontology of facts, version 2: a revision of version 1 (facts.ont), admitted with its mapping (facts-1-to-2.rev). What happens
# is an event; parties' commitments are institutional facts and their contributions events that may honour them; a benefit is an
# evaluation; a social fact must now say how firmly it stands; whether a fact is general is no longer recorded; a construal is no
# longer linked to a trace directly. An example of how an ontology is revised, invented to exercise the revision.
ontology facts version 2
registers Manifestational < Relational < Ontological

kind Fact in Ontological
kind Condition under Fact
kind Event under Fact
kind Contribution under Event
kind Trace under Fact in Manifestational
kind Relational under Fact in Relational
kind Social under Relational
kind Proposal under Social
kind Institutional under Social
kind Uptake under Institutional
kind Decision under Institutional
kind Commitment under Institutional
kind Normative under Institutional
kind Policy under Normative
kind Standard under Normative
kind Rule under Normative
kind Finding under Relational
kind Construal under Finding
kind Estimate under Finding
kind Evaluation under Finding
kind Benefit under Evaluation

ordered axis standing : proposed < claimed < admitted < established
ordered axis grade : C < B < A
axis source : authority, convention, analyst, data, proposal
applies standing to Finding, Social
applies grade to Trace
applies source to Construal
requires grade for Trace
requires standing for Finding, Social

relation rests_on : Finding -> Trace
relation takes_up : Uptake -> Evaluation
relation authorised_by : Institutional -> Rule
relation honours : Contribution -> Commitment
